INT. FEDERAL COURTHOUSE - JURY DELIBERATION ROOM - DAY

A long oak table. Water pitcher. A window with the blinds half drawn. Legal pads scattered like leaves.

Four jurors remain: a retired schoolteacher, a night-shift nurse, a mechanic, and a young law student. They have been here since nine.

AUGUSTINE "GUS" PELLETIER, 60s, retired teacher, sits at the head with his reading glasses in his lap. DELPHINE OKAFOR-ROSS, 40s, nurse, rubs her temples. MARCUS "BUZZ" TEMPLETON, 50s, mechanic, leans back until his chair creaks. And ISADORA FLINT, 20s, law student, sits ramrod straight, pen poised.

                    GUS
We've been going in circles.

                    BUZZ
We've been going in squares.

                    DELPHINE
Buzz, please.

                    BUZZ
I'm just saying. Different shape.

                    ISADORA
Gus, can I propose something?

                    GUS
Of course, Isadora.

                    ISADORA
We take a fresh vote. Anonymous. Written.

                    BUZZ
We did that.

                    ISADORA
We did a show of hands.

                    BUZZ
Same thing.

                    ISADORA
It really isn't.

Delphine tears four slips from a legal pad. Passes them around. Pens scratch. Slips are folded and slid across the table.

Gus gathers them. Unfolds one. Another. Reads. His eyebrows lift.

                    GUS
Three guilty. One not.

                    BUZZ
Who's the holdout?

                    DELPHINE
It's anonymous, Buzz.

                    BUZZ
I can count. It's you, Delphine.

                    DELPHINE
It is not.

                    BUZZ
Then it's you, Isadora.

                    ISADORA
I voted guilty.

                    BUZZ
So did I. So that leaves...

All three turn slowly to look at Gus. He sets down the slips.

                    GUS
Yes.

                    BUZZ
You?

                    GUS
Me.

                    DELPHINE
Gus, the evidence--

                    GUS
Was very good. I know.

                    ISADORA
Then why--

                    GUS
Because I taught that boy. Fifteen years ago.

Silence. The window blinds tick against the sill.

                    BUZZ
You never said.

                    GUS
I wasn't asked.

                    ISADORA
That's grounds for a mistrial.

                    GUS
Possibly.

                    DELPHINE
You should tell the judge.

                    GUS
I will.

                    BUZZ
(muttering)
Well, hell.

Gus stands. His chair scrapes. He takes his glasses from his lap and walks to the door, one hand on the frame.

                    GUS
Delphine, Buzz, Isadora. You have been fair. Thank you.

He knocks twice on the door. The lock clicks. A BAILIFF opens it.

                    GUS (CONT'D)
Bailiff. Please tell Judge Hanratty I need a word.

The door closes behind him. The three jurors sit in silence.

                    ISADORA
Well.

                    DELPHINE
Well.

                    BUZZ
Anyone want a sandwich?

Delphine laughs, once, surprised. Isadora, despite herself, smiles. Buzz pushes back his chair, stretches, and the three of them let out a breath they have been holding all day.
